use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Announcement:
    description:
        Announcement tells assistive tools, such as screen readers, about a
        change without moving focus to it. A message like "3 results" or
        "Added to basket" is read out while the user carries on where they
        were. It belongs to the component where the change happens.

    urgency:
        Most announcements are polite. They wait until the screen reader has
        finished what it is saying. Urgent ones, such as an error that stops
        the user's work, interrupt it. The component decides which, and keeps
        interrupting for things the user must hear straight away.

    message:
        An announcement is short, plain text that makes sense on its own, such
        as "Copied" rather than a tick. It says what happened, not what it
        looks like.

    repeats:
        The same message announced twice is read out twice. A new message
        replaces one that hasn't been read out yet.

    bursts:
        When messages come in quick bursts, such as a result count changing
        with every letter typed, the user hears the latest, not every one. How
        long to wait for things to settle is up to the chosen library.

    regions:
        Some components are themselves a place where messages appear, such as
        a notification area or a status line. New content added there is
        announced as it arrives. Content that was already there when the page
        loaded is not.

    visibility:
        An announcement doesn't have to be shown on screen. A message meant
        only for assistive tools is hidden from view but still read out.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't move focus. Something the user must deal with before
            carrying on needs a blocking panel, not an announcement
        - No history. Messages the user missed aren't kept to be read again,
            so anything they may need later also stays on screen
